  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 112 PM CDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\nA narrow band of mid-level frontogenesis and associated \nsprinkles/light showers will continue to shift south of the \nregion through late this afternoon. \n\nTomorrow, a shortwave at the leading edge of an advancing EML \nplume will slide across the region and interact with a secondary\nwave well to our south. While the main shortwave looks fairly \nrobust, it will be accompanied by sharply-warming and drying \nconditions in the 700-600 mb layer leading to increasing \nconvective inhibition. Moisture looks fairly shallow, likely \ntrapped under 750 mb, but sufficient to support some sprinkles \nor spotty showers Saturday morning, mostly near/south of I-80. \nThis activity will push east of the region through early \nafternoon. Forecast soundings suggest we'll likely hold onto at \nleast some Cu/Stratocu through the day, but should see at least \nsome clearing, particularly west of the Fox Valley. \n\nLow-level moisture will increase through Saturday afternoon and\novernight as the near-surface flow turns south to \nsouthwesterly. Might see some patchy/shallow fog development \ntowards Sunday morning as the increased BL moisture surges \nnorthward, although current soundings show fairly robust flow \ndown low which will probably limit the areal extent. Finally, \nthere's been a modest signal for a disorganized convective \ncomplex developing well to our north (and perhaps a second area \nacross Iowa) Saturday evening. While deep layer flow and \nthicknesses orientations would support a general southeast to \nsouth motion, the environment looks incredibly strongly capped \nand would likely require an intense/mature MCS to result in more\nsignificant convective chances in our area towards early Sunday\nmorning. Not seeing a strong signal for this at this time, and \nas a result, will continue with the precip-free forecast during \nthis period. \n\nDewpoints are likely to push into the upper 70s Sunday \nafternoon with generally muted mixing owing to the presence of a\nfairly robust inversion near 850 mb. The initial moist layer \nlooks pretty shallow though, so may be prone to mixing out a \ntouch, mainly away from agricultural areas. Air temperatures \nwill likely only top out in the upper 80s/near 90 which will \nresult in peak heat indices around 100 to locally above 105, \nparticularly across our south and west locales. In the absence \nof widespread thunderstorms, the potential for greater coverage \n105 degree heat indices exists on Monday, perhaps locally up to \n110 near dense agricultural areas where ET is maximized. Breezy \nsouthwesterly winds may help take some of the impacts of the \nheat off a bit, but either way, Monday looks unpleasantly hot \nand humid. \n\nOur position along the northeast periphery of an expansive high\ncentered across the Four Corners region will open the potential\nfor convective complexes rolling across the northern Great \nPlains and Upper Midwest Sunday into Monday. That said, the \nsignal for such MCSs is not particularly high across the \nguidance suite. While several low-amplitude perturbations are \nevident in the region, in general, capping looks formidable in \nthe region with a very hot/dry EML base overhead. Can't rule out\nthe potential for a small undiagnosed ripple in the flow to set\noff the MCS cascade (latest NAM/NAMNest output on Sunday \nafternoon for example), but this isn't something we can \nmeaningfully anticipate more than a day out. There is somewhat \nof a better signal for convection in or very near our CWA late \nSunday afternoon into Monday morning, but it's also entirely \npossible activity skirts the area to the east. Slight chance to \nchance PoPs will cover this potential.\n\nA cold front Monday afternoon/Monday night will bring another \nthreat for storms (some of which could be severe) to the area, \nbut again this signal is not particularly robust at this point. \nSomewhat less hot/humid conditions will return during the middle\nof next week.\n\nCarlaw",
